oesn't work  in part because there's often informal  dynamics so the formal rules may say yes  let's add more women but the informal  rules the gatekeeping of who gets to run  for office who has resources to run for  office these things that may not  systematically show up but we know  enough anecdotal evidence across the  across the world indicate that there's  other barriers they may be invisible  that still really keep women out of  positions of power yeah and some of them  I find are much more nuanced than maybe  the discussion allows for I mean I'll  sort of give one example you hear from  some contingence political contingents  in the United States now who say listen  this this just simply isn't the problem  women can now vote women can run for  office there's no practically  practically speaking any barriers to  having more women become elected  officials but then you look at a sort of  sort of second layer proxy to that and  you say well overwhelmingly the voting  public is choosing from candidates who  are disproportionately high-powered  attorneys CEOs of businesses and  whatever else right we could figure out  sort of what the top occupations are you  then need to sort of look at what is the  balance or imbalance within those sort  of agent careers that are most likely to  lead to becoming an elected official  would that be one example that's  actually a great example and we call it  a you know political pipeline right and  it's true for representation  there's pipelines also that push women  into different types of jobs within the  economy and there's also a pipeline in  seeking justice so I talk a lot about  these pipelines that that they're often  leaky so there's things along the way  where women sort of fall out of that  pipeline and and your example is it  starts with what kind of occupations  they have where they go to school what  sort of Rhys  sources they start out with right to  become a political representative is a  very elite position in and of itself so  it already is going to exclude most  women and most Americans what do you  think right now is from a sort of  structural standpoint maybe I mean I'm  glad to talk about the u.s. specifically  but we could talk more broadly or you  could talk about other areas where  you've done research when people who  don't believe that there are any sort of  structural inequalities remaining what  are the sort of best examples you can  point to as to what some of those  structural inequalities are there's this  common refrain now from a certain  political segment which says I'm as  against structure structural  inequalities when it comes to race  gender ethnicity whatever as much as the  next guy or gal but nobody's presented  me with a good example of what one would  be what would an example be um well I  think in terms of the economy right that  these offer great examples that many  occupations are still sex segregated  right so but there's there's no formal  rules that that women shouldn't go into  particular fields like construction or  mining or that men can't go into fields  like nursing or primary education but  but that there's forces at play that  sort of push people in particular ways  and that sort of invisible pushing is an  indication that that there's larger  processes going on besides just  individual decision making and and I  think that's the best way to capture  what it means to say something is  structural and then those jobs then are  paid very differently and that has to do  with how we value work which again is  also structural I don't know if that  sort of explains what you're trying to  get at or not I mean I guess it would be  great for for the audience's purposes to  now say okay we understand the idea of  what you're saying but what are some  specific examples I mean I know  generally you're talking about  construction nursing etc but one where  we could actually talk through the  mechanism that's taking place  so let's think in terms of 